 The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
 
+## Developer diagnostics widget
+
+The homepage includes independent browser-side health checks for a hardware test
+device and a local companion CLI. Defaults are examples and can be edited:
+
+- Hardware: `http://192.168.1.100:8080/health` (private IPv4 LAN addresses).
+- CLI: `http://127.0.0.1:4318/health` (loopback IPv4, `localhost`, or `[::1]`).
+
+The services must expose a read-only GET endpoint returning a 2xx status; no
+response body format is required. The device must listen on its LAN interface,
+and the companion must listen on loopback. This repository does not install or
+start either service. Configure each service to return
+`Access-Control-Allow-Origin` with the exact website origin (scheme, host, and
+port; no path), including on error responses. Requests omit credentials and do
+not follow redirects. No application proxy or network discovery is involved.
+
+Serve the site in a secure context (HTTPS or localhost). The fetch requests set
+`targetAddressSpace` to `local` for hardware and `loopback` for the CLI. Supporting
+browsers may prompt for network permissions; denied access can be changed in site
+settings. Browser support and mixed-content handling vary, so a trusted HTTPS
+service endpoint may be required. See the
+[Chrome Local Network Access guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/security/local-network-access).
+
+Checks run only after user interaction and time out after 10 seconds. “Reachable”
+means the last check succeeded, not that a persistent connection is open.
+Disconnect clears that result, and Cancel aborts an in-flight check. Failed HTTP
+responses are distinguished from timeouts; browser network and CORS errors share
+troubleshooting guidance because fetch does not reliably distinguish them.
+
 ## Tech Stack
 
 - **[Astro](https://astro.build/)**: The web framework for building content-driven websites.
